    "narrative": "For the May 30 slate, Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **James Wood**, our two strongest starts with scores of 72.62 and 64.75, respectively. **Vinnie Pasquantino** commands the highest confidence rating today, benefiting from a massive 14% dynamic platoon boost against Rocker, whose 4.30 xERA offers a exploitable ceiling. Simultaneously, **James Wood** is in a prime position to stabilize our stolen base category; despite the proximity of King, his leadoff placement and impressive 0.384 xwOBA make him a priority start even against a high-hold pitcher. **Josh Naylor** also anchors the core at 64.77, as his matchup against Nelson provides a strong, high-floor opportunity to collect counting stats in the heart of the order.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench centered on **Seiya Suzuki**, whose 50.44 rating barely kept him out of the starting OF slots behind **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Henry Bolte**. While **Seiya Suzuki** projects well, the positive park adjustment for **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Henry Bolte** against Weathers proved to be the deciding factor for our optimizer. Elsewhere, our aggressive pursuit of speed remains the priority; **Corbin Carroll** secures a spot at 60.40 despite a tough matchup against Woo, as his xwOBA of 0.373 and potential for a stolen base outweigh the risk of the tough opposing arm. We continue to monitor the status of **Mickey Moniak** and **Giancarlo Stanton**, both of whom remain sidelined, while **Austin Riley** remains on the bench due to his absence from today's active lineup. Users should remain cautious of the volatility surrounding **Gabriel Moreno** and **Cole Young**, as their scores are hampered by SP-related headwinds; however, they remain in the lineup today to preserve our positional flexibility while we await further updates on depth pieces like **Trevor Story**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Kyle Karros**, **Carson Williams**, and **Alex Freeland**.",
